"""Restore closed-book test knowledge and diversify Topic 8 demand."""
CHANGES={}
def add(k,p,a,g,recall=0,sub='PROC',ao='C',bloom='Apply',objectives=None):
 CHANGES[k]=dict(prompt=p,canonical_answer=a,marking_guidance=g.split('|'),recall=recall,subtype=sub,assessment_objectives=[ao],blooms_level=bloom)
 if objectives:CHANGES[k]['objective_ids']=objectives
add('8-015','Describe the flame-test colours of lithium and sodium compounds.','Lithium gives a red flame; sodium gives a bright-yellow flame.','Lithium: red flame (1).|Sodium: bright-yellow flame (1).',2,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
add('8-016','Describe two headings, beyond sample identity, needed in a results table for comparing repeated flame tests of lithium samples. The table must distinguish the flame observation from any pre-existing sample colour.','Use a column headed “flame colour”, rather than simply “colour”, and identify each trial or repeat so separate observations are retained.','A clearly labelled flame-colour observation column, distinct from sample colour (1).|A trial/repeat identifier or separate repeat columns, so repeated observations are recorded independently (1).')
add('8-018','Describe the conclusions from three flame-test repeats. Reference sodium colour: bright yellow. Sample P gives yellow, yellow, yellow; sample Q gives red, yellow, red under the same conditions.','P gives a reproducible result consistent with sodium. Q is inconsistent, so its single yellow result is not enough for a dependable identification.','P consistently matches the supplied sodium reference (1).|Q is inconsistent; one yellow trial cannot support a dependable identification (1).',0,'INTERP','B','Analyse')
add('8-019','Describe the contrasting flame colours expected from potassium and barium compounds.','Potassium gives lilac; barium gives green.','Potassium: lilac (1).|Barium: green (1).',2,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
add('8-020','Explain why a yellow flame does not exclude potassium when a sample may also contain sodium. The sodium flame is much brighter than the potassium flame.','The bright sodium emission can dominate the visible colour and mask the weaker lilac potassium flame, so absence of visible lilac is not evidence that potassium is absent.','Brighter sodium colour can mask the weaker potassium colour (1).|Failure to see potassium colour therefore cannot establish absence of potassium (1).',0,'MECH','A','Understand')
add('8-021','Describe the flame colour of a calcium compound and state the physical feature being observed in a flame test.','Calcium gives a brick-red flame. It is the flame colour being recorded, not the colour of a solution or precipitate.','Calcium: brick-red (1).|Colour belongs to the flame, rather than a solution or precipitate (1).',2,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
add('8-022','Describe two improvements to this calcium flame-test record before comparing repeat trials: “Test looked red. The room lights were changed between trials.”','Record the precise flame shade, distinguishing brick-red from a general red description, and keep viewing conditions or lighting the same between trials.','Record the actual flame shade precisely rather than the vague word red (1).|Keep illumination/viewing conditions constant for a fair comparison (1).')
add('8-023','Describe the identification supported by this two-stage result and how it resolves the original uncertainty. A sample first gave an indistinct greenish flame. References: barium sulfate forms an insoluble white precipitate; potassium sulfate remains soluble. Adding dilute sulfate solution to a fresh sample gives a white precipitate.','Barium is supported. The precipitation result distinguishes it from the supplied potassium alternative, whereas the indistinct flame alone did not.','Barium supported by the white sulfate precipitate (1).|The fresh-portion result discriminates from soluble potassium sulfate and resolves the ambiguous flame (1).',0,'INTERP','B','Analyse')
add('8-024','Describe how to arrange a comparison of barium flame-test repeats when one salt is tested as a large wet lump and the other as a small dry sample. Do not infer identity from differences in brightness.','Use small comparable quantities prepared consistently and keep flame position/viewing conditions consistent; compare the characteristic colour rather than brightness alone.','Make sample amount/preparation comparable, avoiding wet-lump versus dry-small-sample differences (1).|Use consistent flame/viewing conditions and compare colour rather than brightness alone (1).')
add('8-037','Describe the chemical test for ammonia gas, including the condition of the indicator paper and its positive change.','Expose damp red litmus paper to the gas; it turns blue.','Damp red litmus paper exposed to the gas (1).|Paper turns blue (1).',2,'PROC','A','Remember')
add('8-039','Describe the test for carbon dioxide and its positive observation.','Bubble the gas through fresh limewater; the limewater becomes milky.','Pass the gas through fresh limewater (1).|Limewater becomes milky/cloudy from a white precipitate (1).',2,'PROC','A','Remember')
# Chlorine's timing interpretation remains separate from closed-book recall in 8-042.
add('8-042','Describe the test for chlorine gas and the final positive observation.','Expose damp litmus paper to the gas; the paper is bleached.','Damp litmus exposed to the gas (1).|Its colour is bleached/removed (1).',2,'PROC','A','Remember')
add('8-043','Describe the test for a small separately collected hydrogen sample and the positive observation.','Under teacher supervision, apply a lighted splint to the small collected sample; a squeaky pop is heard.','Lighted splint applied to a small separately collected sample (1).|Squeaky pop (1).',2,'PROC','A','Remember')
add('8-045','Describe the test for oxygen and its positive observation.','Insert a glowing splint into the gas; the splint relights.','Glowing, non-flaming splint (1).|Splint relights (1).',2,'PROC','A','Remember')
add('10-032','State the structural feature of CH2=CHCH3 responsible for its rapid reaction with bromine water.','The carbon–carbon double bond (C=C).','The carbon–carbon double bond (1).',1,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
add('12-014','Calculate the delivered volume from burette readings of 1.20 cm³ initially and 24.80 cm³ finally, when both readings are 0.20 cm³ too high.','Corrected readings are 1.00 cm³ and 24.60 cm³. Delivered volume = 24.60 − 1.00 = 23.60 cm³; the equal offsets cancel.','23.60 cm³; accept subtraction of either the two corrected readings or the two recorded readings, since equal offsets cancel (1).',0,'CALC','B','Apply',['OBJ-6117-12.2-02'])
# A closed-book nitrate-test identification is factual recall, even though the old ID said INTERP.
add('8-034','State the anion indicated when a sample confirmed free of ammonium gives ammonia on warming with NaOH and aluminium foil.','Nitrate, NO3⁻.','Nitrate / NO3⁻ (1).',1,'FEATURE','A','Remember')
def apply(x,key):
 if key not in CHANGES:return None
 c=CHANGES[key]
 for k,v in c.items():
  if k!='recall':x[k]=v
 return c['recall']
